1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,592
Your server is running, and it holds data that matters.

2
00:00:03,612 --> 00:00:05,870
Now it needs more space, and some swap.

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:09,959
And you must add them without breaking anything that is already there.

4
00:00:10,429 --> 00:00:15,401
In this video: add partitions, logical volumes, and swap, non-destructively.

5
00:00:19,921 --> 00:00:22,504
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,974 --> 00:00:27,967
Add new partitions and logical volumes, and swap to a system non-destructively.

7
00:00:28,437 --> 00:00:33,539
We'll add a partition, grow a volume group, make a new logical volume, and add swap,

8
00:00:33,559 --> 00:00:36,161
while a file on the system stays untouched.

9
00:00:45,681 --> 00:00:49,672
Non-destructively means: nothing that already exists is erased.

10
00:00:49,692 --> 00:00:53,810
You only use space that is free, or a disk that is new and blank.

11
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:05,964
The dangerous commands are the ones that write a new table or a new file system on

12
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:11,738
something that is in use. Before every step, check with lsblk and vgs what is free.

13
00:01:19,208 --> 00:01:24,389
Today we have a volume group in use, with a mounted volume and a file in it.

14
00:01:24,409 --> 00:01:27,949
And a new blank disk, vdc. We will add three things:

15
00:01:27,969 --> 00:01:32,329
a partition for LVM, a new logical volume, and a swap partition.

16
00:01:40,849 --> 00:01:45,660
First we look. The file in our mounted volume says customer data:

17
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,187
this is the data we must not lose.

18
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:52,052
vgs shows one volume group with four gigabytes free.

19
00:01:52,072 --> 00:01:57,106
And lsblk on vdc shows a disk of five gigabytes, with nothing on it.

20
00:02:06,626 --> 00:02:09,382
On the blank disk, we create a GPT label.

21
00:02:09,402 --> 00:02:12,701
This is safe here only because the disk is empty:

22
00:02:12,721 --> 00:02:15,545
it would erase the table of a disk in use.

23
00:02:21,015 --> 00:02:23,723
Then two partitions, with mkpart.

24
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:29,179
The first, called pv, from one megabyte to two gigabytes, for LVM.

25
00:02:29,199 --> 00:02:35,379
The second, called swap, for swap space, five hundred and twelve megabytes.

26
00:02:35,399 --> 00:02:37,528
lsblk shows vdc1 and vdc2.

27
00:02:48,048 --> 00:02:52,230
The first partition becomes a physical volume with pvcreate,

28
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:54,612
and vgextend adds it to our group.

29
00:02:54,632 --> 00:02:59,234
vgs now shows two physical volumes, and nearly six gigabytes free.

30
00:02:59,254 --> 00:03:02,876
The old volume was never touched, and stays mounted.

31
00:03:13,396 --> 00:03:18,886
Now a new logical volume, one gigabyte, called lvnew, from the free space.

32
00:03:18,906 --> 00:03:24,991
lvs lists both volumes. The Attr column of lvapp has an o, which means it is open:

33
00:03:25,011 --> 00:03:28,416
it is mounted and in use. We did not touch it.

34
00:03:37,936 --> 00:03:44,146
Now swap. Mkswap prepares the second partition as swap space, and prints its UUID.

35
00:03:44,166 --> 00:03:46,577
Swapon switches it on right now.

36
00:03:53,047 --> 00:03:55,626
Swapon dash dash show lists active swap:

37
00:03:55,646 --> 00:03:58,809
our partition, five hundred and twelve megabytes.

38
00:04:05,279 --> 00:04:08,282
To keep it after a reboot, a line in fstab:

39
00:04:08,302 --> 00:04:12,219
the UUID, the word none, the type swap, and the options.

40
00:04:12,239 --> 00:04:16,437
Swap has no folder to mount on, so the second field is none.

41
00:04:25,957 --> 00:04:29,393
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

42
00:04:30,913 --> 00:04:34,572
We reboot. Swap is active again, the new volume is there,

43
00:04:34,592 --> 00:04:38,445
and the old file still says customer data. Nothing was lost.

44
00:04:47,965 --> 00:04:54,191
Your turn. A volume group called vgexam is in use, with data in slash mnt slash keep.

45
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:59,996
Create a GPT label on vdc and a partition of five hundred and twelve megabytes.

46
00:05:00,016 --> 00:05:03,523
Make it swap, switch it on, and add it to fstab.

47
00:05:03,543 --> 00:05:09,108
Add a logical volume called examnew, two hundred megabytes. Run the checker.

48
00:05:16,578 --> 00:05:18,328
Try before you open the solution.

49
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:30,335
Next up: create, mount, unmount, and use VFAT, ext4, and XFS file systems.

50
00:05:30,805 --> 00:05:33,707
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
